cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it as below. When you report a
  measurement, cite the DOI of the version you actually ran rather than the
  concept DOI — the tool's own report records which version produced it, and a
  citation that does not pin the version cannot be checked against that record.
title: "closure_drift: does your version label name exactly one version of your code?"
abstract: >-
  A read-only, zero-dependency tool that measures, over any git repository,
  whether a declared version label identifies exactly one state of the
  producing code at the points where that repository publishes. Distinguishes
  release-based publication (tags) from continuous publication (commits),
  because measuring at every commit reports drift in every repository and
  therefore carries no information. Each report stamps the commit measured and
  the hash of the tool that measured it. The measurement script was
  byte-identical across 0.3.0, 0.4.0, 0.5.0 and 0.6.0 and changed in 0.7.0:
  what changed there is a refusal and not a measurement, and every result
  produced under those earlier deposits remains valid and comparable, each
  report carrying the hash of the detector that produced it. 0.7.1 corrects the
  citation file and the README's version section, which still named 0.6.0 after
  the 0.7.0 release, and makes the deposited fixture find the detector deposited
  beside it. 0.7.0 was tagged as source and never deposited.
